      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the bechamel: melt butter in a small saucepan over medium heat. Add flour and stir for 1 minute to make a roux."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the milk gradually, whisking constantly, until you have a smooth, thick sauce. Season with salt, pepper, and nutmeg. Remove from heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the grill to high. Toast the bread lightly on both sides."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Butter one side of each slice. Spread mustard on one slice if using. Layer ham and half the Gruyere between two slices to make each sandwich."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place sandwiches on the baking tray. Spoon bechamel generously over the top of each. Scatter remaining Gruyere over the sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Grill for 4-5 minutes until the cheese is bubbling and turning golden in patches. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Bechamel has lumps",
              "cause": "Milk added too fast or flour not fully cooked in the roux",
              "fix": "Pass through a sieve and whisk vigorously over low heat to smooth it out"
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Cheese topping is pale after grilling",
              "cause": "Grill not hot enough or sandwich placed too low",
              "fix": "Place rack at top position under the grill and wait for grill to be fully hot before adding sandwiches"
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Bread is soggy under the bechamel",
              "cause": "Sauce applied to untoasted bread",
              "fix": "Always toast both sides before adding the topping"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The croque monsieur appeared in Paris cafes in the early 1900s. The name translates loosely as mister crunch. It was a fast, filling lunch for workers and has stayed on French cafe menus ever since. The croque madame is a close relative, topped with a fried egg. Both versions appear on chalkboard menus across France from breakfast through to late afternoon."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
